Policy definition:

A medication is ANTICOAGULANT if its primary action is to inhibit the
coagulation cascade -- vitamin K antagonists, direct oral anticoagulants
(factor Xa and direct thrombin inhibitors), heparins including low molecular
weight heparins, and related parenteral anticoagulants.

Antiplatelet agents are NOT anticoagulants for this policy; they are a distinct
pharmacological class. Agents that reverse anticoagulation, or that promote
clot stability, are also OTHER.

